## ABSTRACT
Porous liquids (PLs) offer the potential to combine the ready handling and mature industry status of liquid absorbents, with the high permanent porosity of metal organic frameworks. To be functional, these nanocomposites need to satisfy a number of performance parameters, such as stability and viscosity of the porogen-solvent combination, avoiding solvent penetration into metal organic framework (MOF) pores, suitable capacities, and kinetics for gas sorption. In this work, we systematically investigate the component materials to elucidate the parametric space where stable photoluminescence (PL) can be generated. In this situation, deeper conclusions were able to be drawn with regard to the influence of hydrophobicity/philicity on the properties of the resulting nanocomposites. Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) were combined with a range of solvents varying in steric bulk, to deliver \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) sorption capacities as high as \( 4.2\ \mathsf{m m o l\cdot g^{-1}} \) at 10 bar. These findings may have broader implications for future investigations of this tantalising field of nanocomposites.
## KEYWORDS
zeolitic imidazolate frameworks, porous liquids, permanent porosity, gas sorption
## 1 Introduction
Since their conception in 2007, porous liquids (PLs) have been presented as the answer to many of the challenges faced in adsorption and absorption separation processes [1–5]. Challenges include fouling, foaming, corrosion, volatile dangerous compound formation and high regeneration energy requirements [6, 7]. PLs may be a good low-energy alternative to the alkanolamine solutions used in carbon dioxide capture processes [7, 8]. They have also attracted interest due to their exceptional physicochemical properties and potential use in homogeneous catalysis, as a novel reaction media, among other applications [1, 3, 9–12]. In essence, PLs combine the fluid properties of solvents with the high adsorption capacities of porous solids, obtained through three synthetic approaches [1, 6, 10, 13, 14]. Type I PLs are neat liquids that consist of a single molecule or compound that is both liquid and permanently porous; Type II PLs are formed where a porous solid (porogen) is dissolved into a solvent that is excluded from the pores; and Type III PLs are established when the porogen is dispersed in a solvent that is sterically hindered from occupying the pores [2–4]. While a few Type I PLs have been reported to date, their challenging physiochemical properties severely limit their applicability [15, 16]. Basically, Type I PLs are made based on corona-canopy method which came from the idea of surface engineering of nanoparticle cores by ionic or covalent grafting. Different oligomers or polymers were often applied as the organic corona-canopy species and the resultant unique flowing fluids were called nanoparticle organic hybrid materials (NOHMs) [17–19]. By the other approaches, PLs seek to combine the favourable adsorption properties of porous solids, namely permanent porosity, high guest capacity, and low regeneration energy costs, with the good thermal conductivity, mass transfer, and the ability of liquids to be circulated within a unit process [2, 3].
