A broad range of porogen materials have been investigated for Type II/III PLs including metal organic frameworks (MOFs), zeolites, porous carbon, silica nanoparticles, and molecular cages [1–6]. Besides, recently three-dimensional (3D) porous solids such as metal-organic polyhedra (MOP), covalent organic frameworks (COFs), porous aromatic frameworks (PAFs) also have been used to make PLs [20–22]. In each case, porogens are prioritised since their cavity window is large enough for guest species to enter the cavity, but too small for solvent molecules. As a subset of MOFs, zeolitic imidazolium frameworks (ZIFs) are particularly interesting for use in PLs as their small pore window enables a wider range of solvents to be considered [23]. Solvents are thus generally selected for their bulk and size, with ionic liquids, liquid polymers, or deep eutectic solvents with large molecular dimensions often chosen to minimise the likelihood of the solvent penetrating the porogen cavity [15, 22, 24, 25]. Previous works have used, ZIF-7 and ZIF-8 focused on different combination of solvents such as glycols, ionic liquids, and bulky oils. Although, permanent porosity formed in all the cases, the
photoluminescence (PL) viscosity increased. Besides, the other important issue with these systems are solvent volatility, and not scalability [2, 11, 26].
