## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterisation
## 3.1.1 Recovered ZIF characterisation
3.1.1 Recovered ZIF characterisationThe most stable MOFs are known to undergo structural changes in solvents, even in conditions significantly milder than that needed for synthesis [45, 46]. Consequently, verification that the solvent had negligible effects on the structural integrity of ZIFs while in dispersion/dissolution, was key to this study. As the characterisation of a porogen structure in solvent would be challenging, each ZIF's stability was investigated by comparing the as-synthesised framework properties with those recovered from each solvent. Confirmation that each recovered ZIF was free of solvent was established by comparing the Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) and results of thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) with that of the as-synthesised ZIF (Figs. S1 and S2 in the ESM). Excluding the likely presence of N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF) in the as-synthesised ZIF-7 and ZIF-62, each of the other samples showed good agreement apart from ZIF-7EX-MIM and ZIF-8EX-MIM. For ZIF-7 after exposure to MIM, the slight mass loss between 25 and \(350\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was not observed, whereas for ZIF-8, the recovered samples showed slower thermal degradation above \(650\ \mathrm{~}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) [47]. FTIR did not indicate any changes to functional groups, suggesting that differences in thermal stability may be due to differences in the structure [48]. Similarly, powder X-Ray diffraction (PKRD) patterns of the ZIFs after recovery generally match that for the as-synthesised material with the exception of ZIF-7 (Fig. S3 in the ESM). Each solvent appears to have slightly impacted crystallinity, whereas the ZIF-7EX-MIM sample appears to include a second crystalline phase. Surface area measurements also show good agreement between the as-synthesised and recovered ZIFs for all samples except for ZIF-7EX-MIM, which shows a \(50\%\) loss in Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area (Fig. 1 and Fig. S4 and Table S2 in the ESM) [25]. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) reveals that while the morphology of each recovered ZIF appears like the as-synthesised material, the size of ZIF-7EX-MIM nanoparticles appears larger than ZIF-7 and each of the other recovered ZIF-7 samples (Fig. S5 in the ESM). When considered together, these results suggest that ZIF-7 is not chemically stable in MIM and forms a second phase that incorporates the solvent molecule. This finding is unexpected as MIM was chosen specifically for its N-methyl group, which should prevent them from exchanging with the framework benzimidazole linkers. Although post-synthetic modification using non-bridging ligands has been used to introduce defects or create core-shell structures in other MOFs, this does not explain the apparent increase in particle size [45, 46, 49]. For each of the other ZIF-solvent combinations studied, the close resemblance between the as
synthesised ZIF and those recovered from solvent confirms that ZIFs are not significantly affected by dispersion/dissolution in the solvents.
## 3.1.2 Stability
After sonication and allowing ZIF-solvent combinations to stand, the dispersion/dissolution of each ZIF-solvent combination was tested using laser light for the appearance of sediment (Fig. 2). The image scale is based on visual observation from the natural settling and Tyndall effect results of each combination that determine more stable combination with higher values. The laser light was used as an easy way of determining whether a mixture is colloidal or not according to the Tyndall effect. ZIF-81%MIM and ZIF-621%MIPB were found to cause minimal scattering of light, whereas the greatest scattering was observed for ZIF-7 dispersed in TEG and DPG. It should be noted that despite the limited scattering, the presence of low sedimentation in the ZIF-621%MIPB dissolution after sonication suggests the size distribution of ZIF-62 and negligible quantity of the ZIF with a larger size. The results indicate that 1, 3, 5-trisopropyl benzene is an especially poor dissolution media for the larger sized ZIF-62 particles (Fig. S6 in the ESM).
On the other hand, no sedimentation was observed for ZIF-81%MIPB, indicating it as a highly stable colloid, and with the
confirmation of empty pores (vide infra), a Type II PLs. Each of the other ZIF-solvent combinations displayed varying levels of scattering and sedimentation behaviour, representing potential Type III PLs. The desirability of each liquid dispersion/dissolution in terms of stability is presented in Fig. 3. From these screening experiments, six investigated ZIF-solvent combinations (specifically ZIF-71%MPEG, ZIF-71%MPEG, ZIF-81%MIPM, ZIF-81%MPEG, ZIF-81%MPEG, and ZIF-621%MIPB) were tested further to investigate what happens in a ZIF-solvent combination that does not precipitate.
## 3.2 Mechanistic insights to design
PLs need to satisfy a number of performance parameters such as stability and viscosity of the ZIF-solvent combination, avoiding solvent penetration into MOF pores, suitable capacities, and kinetics for gas sorption to be functional. The influence of component materials' buoyancy, gravity, and hydrophobicity/philicity on the properties of the resulting ZIF-solvent combinations were systematically investigated to elucidate the parametric space where functional PLs can be generated. These findings' mechanistic implications may stimulate new thinking in almost any area that uses liquid solvents to be substitute with PLs.
## 3.2.1 Viscosity
The ZIFs in the solvents act as obstacles, hindering the flow of solvent and thus increasing the flow resistance. Therefore, the liquid dispersion/dissolution viscosities are higher than pure solvents (Fig. S7 and Table S3 in the ESM). Despite the increasing viscosity of the ZIF-solvent combinations, ZIF-8-MIM and ZIF-62-TPB dissolution viscosities are still low (3.78 and 6.29 cP, respectively), which can be attributed to the high compatibility between ZIFs and solvents. Nevertheless, for the other samples especially those containing TEG, the viscosity increment is higher.
## 3.2.2 Density
A lower measured than predicted density value is evidence of additional porosity within the composite, i.e., that there are additional pore spaces in the PL composite [50]. Measured densities versus predictions are shown in Fig. 4. Although very similar, the measured values are below the confidence interval for the predictions, confirming this additional porosity. Similar trends were seen in all samples (Fig. S8 and Table S4 in the ESM), although the difference became smaller when linear solvents (such as TEG) were employed. This aligns with the expectation that the smaller steric bulk would increase the propensity for pore penetration.
## 3.2.3 PALS
Positron annihilation lifetime spectroscopy (PALS) gives a unique insight into the pore architecture within nanoporous materials.
Ortho-positronium (o-Pl) is formed within void spaces, the lifetime of this species priors to annihilation with an electron of opposite spin being related to the size of the void space, with relative concentrations also obtainable. As shown in Fig. 5, samples of ZIF-8 and ZIF-62 in their previously identified solvent partners were examined as a function of ZIF loading. These PALS results align strongly to the density findings above and provide solid weight to the proposed structural mechanism (vide infra, Fig. 7). In both samples under examination, the number of void spaces (intensity) increases with ZIF loading. In line with this, we witnessed lifetimes are slightly shorter in all instances than that expected for a simple additive mixture. Taken together, these results indicate that the nanocomposites have more, and smaller, void apertures on average than expected from the constituent parts.
## 3.2.4 High-pressure gas sorption
PLs may possess significant sorption capacities due to the presence of the permanent porosity, which can provide additional free volume. As presented in Fig. 6 and Fig. S10 in the ESM,
these observations correlate well with the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption measurements for the PLs. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption capacities of the ZIF-based PLs were as much as 4 times larger than the pure solvents. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption of MIM and ZIF-8-MIM at 10 bar were 2.4 and \(4.2\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, \(75\%\) higher than the native materials. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption of TPB at 10 bar was \(1.0\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and ZIF-62-TPB showed a \(30\%\) enhancement \((1.3\;\mathrm{mmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})\). The improvements in gas adsorption provide enough evidence for the presence of guest accessible porosity in the solvents of all the examined samples.
## 4 Competitive hydrophilicity/phobicity interactions
Interestingly, despite the relatively similar chemical properties of the ZIFs studied, the stability of dispersions/dissolutions prepared with each solvent varies significantly. The external surface of each ZIF includes a mix of \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) ions and imidazole species. Imperfect surface termination of the alternating framework structure (biasing either the metal ion or ligand species) is expected to result in an electrostatic attraction causing the adjacent ZIF nanoparticles to aggregate. Therefore, the interaction of ZIF to adjacent particles and their flotation or sedimentation in the solvent must be overcome to prepare a stable colloid [26]. Furthermore, when ZIFs are present in a solvent, all the possible interactions are strongly dependent on the surrounding solvent [50].
As presented in Fig. 7(a), the hydrophobic TPB solvent can effectively solvate the organic ligands, including imidazole and particularly benzimidazole on the ZIF-62 surface due to the high compatibility with the hydrophobic groups. Solvation produces electron cloud and long-range charge density oscillations around ZIF-62, which leads to steric repulsion, preventing interpenetration. Additionally, weak van der Waals attractions between \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) ions of the ZIF-62 and the benzene electrons can cause a better dissolution [26, 42, 51–53]. While the MIM as a hydrophilic solvent can weakly solvate 2-methylimidazole as the ZIF-8 organic ligand, it possesses adequate nucleophilicity and strong chemical binding affinity toward ZIF-8 ions (Fig. 7(b)). This means that, very efficient van der Waals interactions between
\(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions of the ZIF-8 and electron-rich nitrogen atoms of MIM species exist at the interface and consequently a stable dissolution formation is established [28, 51, 52].
Then, when two ZIF-62 or ZIF-8 particles become very close to each other, the van der Waals forces arise as repulsive forces between particles. This is due to the ZIF-62 particles being solvated, or ZIF-8 particles chemically binding with the solvent more strongly than with the other particle, and it leads to stable colloids [51]. Although both solvation and chemical binding play important roles in preventing ZIF aggregation, the latter usually is predominant [26]. Indeed, when chemical bonding affinities exist between ZIF ions and solvent species at the interface, a very stable colloid can be formed over a long period without any aggregation or sedimentation [52].
Thus, ZIF-62-TPB and ZIF-8-MIM systems lead to stable Type II PLs. Although the solvent dispersion with TEG possesses more interaction than solvent dispersion with DPG, the solvation has weaker behaviour. Moreover, ZIF-8 has more interaction with TEG than ZIF-7, while ZIF-7 solvation with DPG should be higher than ZIF-8. These effects aid dissolution and stability [2, 51].
## 5 Conclusion
The ideal PL would be stable, non-volatile, and free-flowing so that the benefits of its fluid properties can be realised with conventional processes. In order to achieve, a fundamental understanding of how the liquids form is critical. In the foregoing we reported that the compatibility of porogen molecules and solvents is influential on stable PL formation. In such stable MOF-solvent combination, the inner porosity of the MOF must remain empty. At the same time, particle-particle interactions must be prevented to avoid agglomeration.
TPB exhibited strong hydrophobic interactions with ZIF-62 and effectively solvated the ZIF-62 surface. The hydrophilic MIM weakly interacts weakly with the organic ligands on the ZIF-8 surface, but interacts strongly with \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\), giving rise to the likewise stable formation in a differing manner.
The significant numbers of MOFs and solvents lead to infinite combinations of MOF-based PLs with potential applications in many fields. The ZIF-8-MIM and ZIF-62-TPB have an exciting combination of stability, low viscosity, and good sorption properties for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) gas scrubbing applications as Type II PLs. They possess strong solvation, adequate nucleophilicity and chemical binding affinity, which led to dissolving ZIFs into solvents. In contrast, the other ZIF-solvent combinations interact more weakly and can be classified as Type III PLs. The crystallinity, chemical bonding, thermal stability, morphology, surface area, and pore size of the as-synthesised ZIFs were not affected by exposure to the solvents. The measured densities of ZIF-based PLs were less than the ideal mixture density. The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) sorption capacities of the ZIFs PLs were up to four times larger than the pure solvents over the entire measured pressure range.
